[Thm strong inequality of arXiv:2412.02913, 'On the boundedness of Gross' solution when the underlying distribution is bounded'] [butzer1971hilbert] If $f$ is in $L_{2\pi}^{p}$, then $H\{f\}$
exists almost everywhere for $p>1$. Furthermore, we have
\begin{equation}
\Vert H\{f\}\Vert_{p}\leq\lambda_{p}\Vert f\Vert_{p}
\end{equation}
for some positive constant $\lambda_{p}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem_new_c_star of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] Under the assumptions of Theorem~(thm:speed.FPP of the paper), there exists~$C^* > 0$ such that almost surely, for every~$x \in \mathbb R^d$ with~$\|x\|$ large enough, we have
    \begin{equation}
    \left| T(x) - \frac{\|x\|}{\upvarphi}\right| \le C^* \sqrt{\|x\|}\log\big(\|x\|\big).
    \end{equation}

metadata_json:
{"artifact_digest":"sha256:bb3b95da376446f7088f6742c2bb4d9023558be1c0b5c4ce82c99f100be11889","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/bb/bb3b95da376446f7088f6742c2bb4d9023558be1c0b5c4ce82c99f100be11889.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:ed463058e8c16e0329ac04d3b49b73553bbe7d84dd5a57a31c0116dcd7c226ba","bundle_entry_key":"2411-02046-lemma-23-lem-new-c-star","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_087","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_087.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2411-02046-lemma-23-lem-new-c-star","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: By putting together Theorem~(thm_new_moderate_deviations of the paper) and~(equation eq_asymp_exp of the paper), we can choose~$C^* > 0$ such that, for all~$x \in \mathbb R^d$ with~$\|x\|$ large enough,
\begin{equation} 
	\mathbb P\left(\left|T(x)-\frac{\|x\|}{\upvarphi}\right| > \frac{C^*}{2} \sqrt{\|x\|} \log(\|x\|)\right) < \|x\|^{-5d}.
\end{equation}

Define
\[Z(x):= \mathds{1}\left\{ \left| T(x) - \frac{\|x\|}{\upvarphi}\right| > C^* \sqrt{\|x\|} \log(\|x\|) \right\}.\]
Our goal is to prove that
\begin{equation}
	\mathbb P \left(\limsup_{\|x\| \to \infty} Z(x) = 1 \right) = 0.
\end{equation}

Let us start by defining the auxiliary random variables
