[Definition 25 (Outgoing eikonal function and inverse foliation density) of arXiv:2609.03101, 'The emergence of the Cauchy horizon from the crease for $3D$ compressible Euler flow'] The \emph{outgoing eikonal function} $u$ is the solution of the following fully nonlinear hyperbolic initial value problem,
where $\gfour$ is the acoustical metric defined in \eqref{E:ACOUSTICALMETRIC}
and the PDE is known as the \emph{acoustic eikonal equation}:
\begin{align} 
\begin{cases}
(\gfour^{-1})^{\alpha \beta} \partial_{\alpha} u \partial_{\beta} u = 0, 
	\\
\p_t u > 0, 
	\\
u|_{\Sigma_0} = - x^1.
\end{cases}
\end{align}
We define the \emph{outgoing inverse foliation density} $\upmu$ by:
\begin{align} 
	\upmu 
	& 
	\eqdef 
	- 
	\frac{1}{(\gfour^{-1})^{\alpha \beta} \partial_{\alpha} t \partial_{\beta} u} > 0.
\end{align}

[Proposition 46 of arXiv:2608.27307, '2608.27307'] Under Assumptions~\ref{ass:basic} and~\ref{ass:rand_ent}, suppose that $E_{+,0}$ is a regular right edge for $\Pi_0$ in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:reg_r_edge}. With high probability, $Y_0$ is $\eta_*$-regular around $\la_{1,0}$ in the sense of \cite[Definition~1]{ding2022edge} or \cite[Definition~1]{ding2022tracywidom}. More explicitly, there exist constants $c,C>0$ such that
        \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
                \item For $z=E+\ii\eta$ with \begin{align}
                        \la_{1,0}-c\leq E\leq\la_{1,0},\qquad \eta_*+\sqrt{\eta_*\abs{\la_{1,0}-E}}\leq\eta\leq10,  
                \end{align} 
                we have
                \begin{align}
                         \Im m_{0,N}(E+\ii\eta) & \sim\sqrt{\la_{1,0}-E+\eta},  
                \end{align}
                \item For $z=E+\ii\eta$ with \begin{align}
                        \la_{1,0}\leq E\leq\la_{1,0}+c,\qquad \eta_*\leq\eta\leq10, 
                \end{align}
                we have
                \begin{align}
                         \Im m_{0,N}(E+\ii\eta) & \sim\frac{\eta}{\sqrt{E-\la_{1,0}+\eta}}. 
                \end{align}
        \end{enumerate}
       Moreover, $\la_{1,0}\sim1$ and $\|W_0\|\leq C$ with high probability. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

