[Corollary cor:explicit-constrained-mgle-barycenter-final of arXiv:2604.22453, 'Adapted Wasserstein Barycenters of Gaussian Processes'] Let \(\mathbb{X}^k=\mathbb{G}^{0,L^k}\in\mathcal{FG}(T,d)\), \(k\in[K]\), be arbitrary centered
filtered Gaussian inputs.  Then the constrained problem
(equation eq:constrained-mgle-barycenter-problem-final of the paper) is solved as follows.  First form the
martingale projections \((L^k)^{\mathrm{mgle}}\) by
(equation eq:canonical-mgle-projection-final of the paper).  Then, for every \(t\in[T]\), choose
\begin{equation}

\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{\Sigma}}}{\Sigma}
_t:=
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
_{t,t}
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
_{t,t}^{\intercal}
\in
\argmin_{\Sigma\in\mathscr S_+(d)}
\sum_{k=1}^K\lambda_k
\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{BW}}^2\!\left(
(L^k)^{\mathrm{mgle}}_{t,t}\big((L^k)^{\mathrm{mgle}}_{t,t}\big)^{\intercal},
\Sigma
\right),
\end{equation}
and set \(
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
_{t,s}=
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
_{s,s}\) for \(t\ge s\). Then
\(\mathbb{G}^{
\accentset{\overline{\hphantom{L}}}{L}
}\in\mathcal{FG}_{\mathrm{mgle}}(T,d)\) solves
(equation eq:constrained-mgle-barycenter-problem-final of the paper).  Its value is
\begin{align}

&\sum_{k=1}^K\lambda_k\|L^k-(L^k)^{\mathrm{mgle}}\|_F^2\notag\\
&\quad+
\sum_{t=1}^T(T-t+1)
\min_{\Sigma_t\in\mathscr S_+(d)}
\sum_{k=1}^K\lambda_k
\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{BW}}^2\!\left(
(L^k)^{\mathrm{mgle}}_{t,t}\big((L^k)^{\mathrm{mgle}}_{t,t}\big)^{\intercal},
\Sigma_t
\right),
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}

(L^k)^{\mathrm{mgle}}_{t,t}
=
\frac{1}{T-t+1}\sum_{s=t}^T L^k_{s,t}.
\end{equation}

[Theorem thm:DE of arXiv:2412.04579, 'Solvable Families of Random Block Tridiagonal Matrices'] Fix $\beta>0$ and an integer $n\ge 1$. Let $a_1,\dots, a_n$, $b_1, \dots, b_{n-1}$ be independent random variables with $a_j\sim N\bigl(0,\frac{2}{\beta}\bigr)$, \smash{$b_j\sim \frac{1}{\sqrt{\beta}}\chi_{\beta (n-j)}$}. Then the symmetric tridiagonal matrix $\mathbf{T}$ with diagonal $a_1,a_2,\dots$ and off-diagonal $b_1,b_2, \dots$ has a joint symmetrized eigenvalue density on ${\mathbb R}^n$ given by
\begin{align*}
 \frac{1}{Z_{n,\beta}} \left|\Delta(\lambda)\right|^\beta {\rm e}^{-\frac{\beta}{4} \sum_{j=1}^n \lambda_j^2},
\end{align*}
with
\begin{align}
 Z_{n,\beta}={n!} (2\pi)^{n/2} (\beta/2)^{-\frac{\beta}{4}n(n-1)-\frac{n}{2}} \Gamma(\beta/2)^{-n} \prod_{j=1}^n \Gamma(\beta j/2).
\end{align}
Moreover, the spectral weights of $\mathbf{T}$ corresponding to the first coordinate vector have joint distribution given by $\mathrm{Dirichlet}(\beta/2,\dots, \beta/2)$, and this weight vector is independent of the eigenvalues.

