[Definition of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] Let $\mathcal{X}$ be a PP in $\mathbb{R}$. Suppose that there exists a function $K:\mathbb{R}^2\rightarrow\mathbb{C}$ such that, for almost every $x_j\in\mathbb{R}$,
\begin{equation*}
    \rho_k(x_1,\dots,x_k)=\det\left(K(x_j,x_l)_{j,l=1}^k\right),
\end{equation*}
holds for any $k\in\mathbb{N}$. Then $\mathfrak{X}$ is called a \textbf{determinantal~point~process}, and $K$ is called the \textbf{kernel} of $\mathfrak{X}$.

[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2606.15178, 'The distribution of the de Moivre experiment'] Let $ s\geq1 $ and $ k\geq0 $ be fixed integers. We have
	\begin{equation}
		\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty} \dfrac{{n \choose k}_s}{n^k}=\dfrac{1}{k!}.
	\end{equation}

