[Definition def-conclave of arXiv:2607.22324, 'The conclave process'] {}
    The random process $\mathbf{X}_k=(\mathbf{X}_k^{(1)},...,\mathbf{X}_k^{(n)})$ for $k \in \mathbb Z_{\geq 0}$ (with size $n$ and weight function $\mu$) is a Markov process on $\Omega_n$ defined by the following evolution:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For $k=0$, set $\mathbf{X}_0:=(1,1,...,1)$. 
        \item For $k\ge 1$, given that $\mathbf{X}_{k-1}=(a_{k-1}^{(1)},...,a_{k-1}^{(n)})\in \Omega_n$, sample i.i.d.\ random variables 
        \begin{align}
            \{\mathbf{B}_{k,j}\}_{j=1}^{n} \mbox{ such that } \mathbb{P}(\mathbf{B}_{k,j}=i)=\frac{\mu(a_{k-1}^{(i)})}{\sum_{1\le l\le n}\mu(a_{k-1}^{(l)})}  
        \end{align}
        for all $i,j\in [n]:=\{1,2,...,n\}$, and then define $\mathbf{X}_{k}^{(i)}:=\sum_{j=1}^{n} \mathbf{1}_{ \{ \mathbf{B}_{k,j}=i \} }$ for $i\in [n]$. 
    \end{itemize}

