[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2609.03961, 'On the exponential sum over squarefree integers'] Let $M\ge1$.  There is a trigonometric polynomial
\begin{equation}
  G_M(x)=\sum_{|h|\le H_M}c_h\e(hx),
  \qquad H_M\le\frac M2,
\end{equation}
with real coefficients satisfying $c_{-h}=c_h$ and
$0\le c_h\le12\log 2M$, such that $G_M(x)\ge0$ and
\begin{equation}
  \min\left(M,\frac1{2\|x\|}\right)\le G_M(x)
\end{equation}
for all real $x$.

[Definition 19 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix a set $\Sigma = \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$ with $N \in \mathbb{N} \cup \{\infty\}$ and $T_0, T_1\in \mathbb{Z}$ with $T_0 \leq T_1$. A $\Sigma$-indexed geometric line ensemble $\mathfrak{L} : \Sigma \times \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ is said to satisfy the {\em interlacing Gibbs property} if it is interlacing, meaning that $L_i \succeq L_{i+1}$ for all $i \in \llbracket 1, N-1 \rrbracket$, and for any finite $K = \llbracket k_1, k_2 \rrbracket \subset \llbracket 1, N - 1 \rrbracket$ and $a,b \in \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket$ with $a < b$ the following holds.  Suppose that $f, g$ are two increasing paths drawn in $\{ (r,z) \in \mathbb{Z}^2 : a \leq r \leq b\}$ and $\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \mathfrak{W}_k$ with $k=k_2-k_1+1$ are such that $\mathbb{P}(A) > 0$, where $A$ denotes the event $$A =\{ \vec{x} = ({L}_{k_1}(a), \dots, {L}_{k_2}(a)), \vec{y} = ({L}_{k_1}(b), \dots, {L}_{k_2}(b)), L_{k_1-1} \llbracket a,b \rrbracket = f, L_{k_2+1} \llbracket a,b \rrbracket = g \},$$
where if $k_1 = 1$ we adopt the convention $f = \infty = L_0$. Then, writing $k = k_2 - k_1 + 1$, we have for any $\{ B_i \in \Omega(a, b, x_i , y_i) \}_{i = 1}^k$ that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\left( L_{i + k_1-1}\llbracket a,b \rrbracket = B_{i} \mbox{ for $i \in \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket$} \, \vert  A \, \right) = \mathbb{P}_{\ice, \operatorname{Geom}}^{a,b, \vec{x}, \vec{y}, f, g} \left( \cap_{i = 1}^k\{ Q_i = B_i \} \right),
\end{equation}
where $\mathfrak{Q} = \{Q_i\}_{i = 1}^k$ has law $\mathbb{P}_{\ice, \operatorname{Geom}}^{a,b, \vec{x}, \vec{y}, f, g}$.

