body:
[Thm 2 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Let $F\in\Cmats{m}{n}(z_1,z_2)$ be a rational  matrix valued function. Then the following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[\rm (i)]
\item $F$ has no poles in $\RHPtwo$, and $F(z_1,z_2)$ is a contraction for any $z_1,z_2\in\RHP$;
\item $F$ admits a realization of the form
\begin{equation}
F(z_1,z_2)= D+ C(z_1P_1+z_2P_2+A)^{-1}B,
\end{equation}
where the block matrix $\matr{A&B\\C&D} \in\Cmats{(k+m)}{(k+n)}$ is such that
\begin{equation}

\matr{
A+A^*- C^*C & C^*D+ B \\ D^* C+ B^*  & I - D^* D
} \geq 0,
\end{equation}
$P_1,P_2\in \sqmat{k}$ are positive semidefinite and $P_1+P_2=I_k$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, if any of the above conditions holds, then
the limit $\lim_{t\to\infty}F(t,t)$ exists.
Additionally, if $F\in\mathbb R^{m\times n }(z_1,z_2)$ then the matrices $A,B,C,D,P_1,P_2$ in {\rm (ii)} may be chosen as real matrices.

