[Lemma lem:prob-one of arXiv:2512.21026, 'Minimax Duality in Game-Theoretic Probability\thanks{Work in progress: mistakes are likely'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{Z})$ be a downward-scalable gamble space, and $A\subseteq\Omega$.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item If there exists $Z^*\in\mathcal{Z}$ bounded from below such that $Z^*(\omega) = \infty$ for all $\omega\notin A$, then $A$ holds g.t.a.s.
  \item For $\alpha \in (0,1]$, if there exists $Z^*\in\mathcal{Z}$ with $Z^* \geq -1$ such that $1 + Z^*(\omega) \geq 1/\alpha$ for all $\omega\in A$, then $\overline{\textsf{\textbf{P}}} A \leq \alpha$.
  \end{enumerate}

[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2609.04095, '2609.04095'] Let \(q,g,W\) be nonnegative integers, and let \(m\ge1\) be an integer.
Then
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 &N^2(N-1)
 \bigl(T_m(W)^2-T_{m-1}(W)T_{m+1}(W)\bigr)\\
 &\hspace{35mm}=(N-1)V^2-NZ\Sigma.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
For these fixed \(W\) and \(m\), the inequality
\eqref{eq:universal-tail} is equivalent to
\begin{equation}
 (N-1)V^2\ge NZ\Sigma.
\end{equation}

