[Question conj:spec-fBm of arXiv:2512.06984, 'Infinite Dimensional Multifractal Analysis of the Wiener measure'] Is the following conjecture true ?  \medskip
        For every $ H^* \in ( 0,1) $, the local order of the fractional Brownian measure $W^{H^*} $  in the uniform norm evaluated at $B^H$ for $ H \in (0,1) $ is $ W^H$-a.s.
        \begin{equation*}
            \mathsf{ord}_{ \mathrm{loc}  } W^{H^*} ( B^H) =
            \begin{dcases}
                \frac{1+ 2 ( H^{*} - H)}{H} & \qquad  \text{ if } \, H < H^* \\
                \frac{1}{H^*} & \qquad  \text{ otherwise.}
            \end{dcases}
        \end{equation*}
    In particular on $ \Omega^\mathrm{crit} $ it would hold: 
    \begin{equation*}
        f_{W^{H^*}}^\mathsf{ord} ( \xi) = 
        \begin{dcases}
        \frac{\xi  + 2  }{ 1 + 2 H^* } & \text{ if } \xi \ge \frac{1}{H^*} \\
             - \infty   & \text{ otherwise. }  
        \end{dcases}\;. 
    \end{equation*} (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)

[Definition 8 (Grassmann--Pl\texorpdfstring{\"u}{u}cker parametrization and neural variety) of arXiv:2609.03361, 'Grassmann--Pl\"ucker Parametrization of Convolutional Filter Subspaces:\ Regularity and Cl'] Define
\begin{equation}
  \Phi=\Pl_H\circ\Gamma_{\cC}:
  \Gr(q,\cK)\longrightarrow\PP(\wedge^qH).
\end{equation}
Its image
\begin{equation}
  Y=\Phi\bigl(\Gr(q,\cK)\bigr)
\end{equation}
is called the Grassmann--Pl\"ucker neural variety of this single-layer
convolutional model.

[Definition 53 of arXiv:2508.04938, 'Perfect t-embeddings of doubly periodic Aztec diamonds'] For~$q=(z,w)\in \mathcal R_0$, we let~$\gamma_q$ be as in Definition~\ref{def:curve}. We define
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal Z(q)=2a\sqrt{a^2+1}+\frac{1}{2\pi\i}\int_{\gamma_q}f(z)Q(z,w)g(z)\d z, \quad \text{and} \quad \vartheta(q)=\frac{1}{2\pi\i}\int_{\gamma_q}f(z)Q(z,w)\bar g(z)\d z,
\end{equation*}
where~$f$ and~$g$ are defined in Corollary~\ref{cor:gauge_2xell},~$\bar g(z)=\overline{g(\bar z)}$, and~$Q$ is given in~\eqref{eq:def_Q}.

