[Definition def:mucs_extend of arXiv:2605.22787, 'Invariant measures for half-space geometric LPP: classification and the one force--one sol'] For $c \in [0,q^{-1})$ and $q^{-1} > s_1 > s_2 > \cdots > s_m \ge r_c$, let $\mu_{c,s_1,\ldots,s_m}$ denote the law of $(f_1,\ldots,f_m)$, where  
    \begin{equation} 
    f_i(k) = \lim_{\varepsilon \searrow 0}[ \mathcal G^\varepsilon\bigl((2m + 1 - i,i),(2m + k,2m)\bigr) - \mathcal G^\varepsilon\bigl((2m + 1 - i,i),(2m,2m)\bigr)], \quad i \in \llbracket 1,m \rrbracket, k \in \mathbb{N}.
    \end{equation}
    We extend the definition of $\mu_{c,s_1,\ldots,s_m}$ for general $(s_i)_{i \in \llbracket 1,m \rrbracket} \subseteq [r_c,q^{-1})$ as follows: If the $s_i$ are all distinct, let $\sigma$ be the permutation of $\llbracket 1,m \rrbracket$ satisfying $s_{\sigma(1)} > s_{\sigma(2)} > \cdots > s_{\sigma(m)}$. Then, define 
    \[\mu_{c,s_1,\ldots,s_m} := \mu_{c,s_{\sigma(1)},\ldots,s_{\sigma(m)}} 
    \circ \sigma^{-1},
    \]
    where $\sigma$ acts on $(f_i)_{i \in \llbracket 1,m \rrbracket}$ as $\sigma (f_i)_{i \in \llbracket 1,m \rrbracket} = (f_{\sigma_i})_{i \in \llbracket 1,m \rrbracket}$.
    If the $s_i$ are not all distinct, then let $(s_{\ell_1},\ldots,s_{\ell_p})$ be a subsequence of maximal length such that the $s_{\ell_i}$ are all distinct. Then, let $\mu_{c,s_1,\ldots,s_m}$ be the law of $(f_1,\ldots,f_m)$, where $(f_{\ell_1},\ldots,f_{\ell_p}) \sim \mu_{c,s_{\ell_1},\ldots,s_{\ell_p}}$, and $f_i = f_j$ whenever $s_i = s_j$.

[Theorem 58 (Intrinsic local-to-global coefficient reconstruction) of arXiv:2606.29292, 'Intrinsic Gaussian Tensor Geometry and Random Operator Spectra'] Fix $m\ge1$ and $1\le r<\infty$.  Let
$(K_{\lambda,\mu})$ be a block kernel family as above and assume
\begin{equation}

 \Rec_{m,r}(\mathbf K)
 :=\left(\sum_{\lambda,\mu}
      \Prof_{m,r}(K_{\lambda,\mu})^{s_r}\right)^{1/s_r}<\infty.
\end{equation}
Then the unordered series
\begin{equation}

 K:=\sum_{\lambda,\mu}K_{\lambda,\mu}
\end{equation}
converges unconditionally in the simultaneous max-cut completion and
\begin{equation}

 \Prof_{m,r}(K)\le\Rec_{m,r}(\mathbf K).
\end{equation}
More precisely, the limit belongs to $\mathfrak T_{m,r}^{\cap}$ when
$1\le r<2$ and to $\mathfrak T_{m,r}$ when $2\le r<\infty$.  Under the
canonical identifications of Part~\ref{part:coefficient-geometry}, it belongs
to the intrinsic coefficient space and satisfies
\begin{equation}

 \|K\|_{\CutCoeff_{m,r}}\le\Rec_{m,r}(\mathbf K).
\end{equation}
For symmetric blocks, the labelled limit belongs to $\Coeff_{m,r}$ and
satisfies the analogous bound with the same right-hand side.

