[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2506.10817, 'On the Weak Error for Local Stochastic Volatility Models'] Let $h,T$ be such that $\frac Th\in \mathbb{N}$, $\sigma$ and $\xi$ satisfy Assumption \ref{Aspt_new} and $f$ be $3$-times weakly differentiable with derivatives of polynomial growth. Assume that $\left(\hE\bigl[\xi_{jh}|\,\hX_{jh}\bigr]\right)_{0\le j\le T/h-1}$ is uniformly bounded from above and below by positive constants not depending on $h$. Then for all $p>1$
    \begin{align*}
        \Bigl|\E\bigl[ f(Y_T)\bigr]-\E\bigl[f(\hX_T^h)\bigr]\Bigr|\lesssim h+\sup_{j\in\{0,\dots,\frac Th-1\}} \bigl\| {\hE\bigl[\xi_{jh}^2\big|\hX_{jh}^h\bigr]}
        -{\E\bigl[\xi_{jh}^2\big|\hX_{jh}^h\bigr]}\bigr\|_{L^p}.
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:reverse_linearized_check_score_representation of arXiv:2608.23938, 'Generalization, memorization, and overfitting \\for diffusion models trained in the lazy h'] For every \(t\in [t_0,T]\), \(\widetilde{\mathcal{K}}_t\) maps \(\widetilde{{\mathcal V}}\) to itself, and the linearized score function \(\widetilde{\boldsymbol S}_t\) admits the representation
    \[
        \widetilde{\boldsymbol S}_t({\boldsymbol x})
        =\frac{1}{\sigma_t}\widetilde{\boldsymbol \Psi}_t{\boldsymbol x}
    \]
    where
    \[
        \widetilde{{\boldsymbol \Psi}}_t :=  
         -\frac{1}{\sigma_t}
        \begin{bmatrix}0&\mathbf{I}_d\end{bmatrix}
        \left(\mathbf{I}_{n+d}-e^{-\mathsf{t}_t\widetilde{{\boldsymbol K}}_t}\right)
        \begin{bmatrix}0\\\mathbf{I}_d\end{bmatrix}, \qquad \widetilde{{\boldsymbol K}}_t :=  \begin{bmatrix}
            \widetilde{\boldsymbol D}_t+\dfrac{\rho_t^2}{dn}{\boldsymbol X}^{\mathsf T}{\boldsymbol X}
            &\dfrac{\rho_t\sigma_t}{d\sqrt n}{\boldsymbol X}^{\mathsf T}\\[1.2ex]
            \dfrac{\rho_t\sigma_t}{d\sqrt n}{\boldsymbol X}
            &\dfrac{\sigma_t^2}{d}\mathbf{I}_d
        \end{bmatrix},
    \]
    and \(\widetilde{\boldsymbol D}_t :=  \operatorname{diag}(\widetilde\delta_{1,t},\ldots,\widetilde\delta_{n,t})\). Furthermore, \(-\sigma_t^{-1}\mathbf{I}_{d}\preceq \widetilde{\boldsymbol \Psi}_t\preceq 0\).

