[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2605.17494, 'Generalized intersection exponents and local cut points for three-dimensional Brownian loo'] Let $\alpha_*\in I_0$, $k \in \mathbb{N}$, and $0<\lambda_0<\lambda_1$. Then there exist
    constants $C_1,C_2>0$, depending only on $\alpha_*$, $k$, $\lambda_0$ and $\lambda_1$, such that
    for all $\alpha\in[0,\alpha_*]$, all $\lambda \in [\lambda_0,\lambda_1]$, and all $r\ge 0$,
    \begin{equation}
        C_1 e^{-r\xi_\alpha (k,\lambda)} \leq p(\alpha,k,r,\lambda) \leq C_2 e^{-r\xi_\alpha (k,\lambda)}.
    \end{equation}
    As a result, \(\xi_\alpha(k,\lambda)\in(0,\infty)\) for all \(\alpha\in I_0\) and \(\lambda>0\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

