{"artifact_digest":"sha256:7892719f161ea7e357fbd4983229f788fc1ca34da3db77b83e2079aded9bebe4","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/78/7892719f161ea7e357fbd4983229f788fc1ca34da3db77b83e2079aded9bebe4.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:df9112b70cc2e03315124009543ec4fb699905336ca477226620fa14a446512f","bundle_entry_key":"2608-29013-lemma-47-lem-47","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_067","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_067.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-29013-lem-47-lem-47","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Note that the chain group $C_n(K, \xi)$ can be written as $\bigoplus_{\tau \in K_n} \mathbb Z \otimes_{\mathbb Z} \mathbb Z\langle \tau \rangle$. 
    Define $\pi_{\sharp}: C_n(L; \mathbf{m}_{\mathbb Z}) \to C_n(K; \mathbb Z)$ by: for any $n$-simplex $\sigma$ of  $L$,
    \begin{equation*}
        \pi_{\sharp}([k\otimes \sigma]) := k \otimes \pi(\sigma). 
    \end{equation*}
    Here, we need $L$ to be regular so that $\pi(\sigma)$ is an $n$-simplex of $K$. 
    It is clear that $\pi_{\sharp}$ is a well defined linear isomorphism. 

