[Lemma lem:R3_integral-2 of arXiv:2605.15545, 'Crossover from subcritical to critical decay: \\ random walk, self-avoiding walk, percolat'] Let $d \ge 1$, $\varepsilon \in (0,1) $, and denote
$\zeta_0 = \zeta \wedge \frac {d \wedge 2}2$.
Let $x\in{\mathbb Z^d}$ be a nonzero vector in the same direction as $\eta$,
i.e., $\eta = \abs \eta \hat x$.
If $d \le 2$, we further assume $\abs x \abs \eta \ge s_0$.
Then when $T \ge \varepsilon^{-1} \vee \varepsilon^{-(2+\zeta_0)/\zeta_0}$ and
$\abs x \ge 4dM / s_0$, we have
\begin{equation} 
\frac{ 1 }{ B(x; \eta )}
	\int_T^\infty \mathrm{d} t\, \abs{ I_{t}(x)
	- \hat g(0) \rho_t(x; \eta,\Lambda) }
\lesssim  e^{-c_1/\varepsilon}  + \frac 1 { \varepsilon^{d+1+\zeta_0} \abs x^{\zeta_0} }
	+ \frac 1 { \varepsilon^{ (d\vee4) + \zeta_0} \abs x^{\zeta_0 / 2} } ,
\end{equation}
with $c_1 = \frac 1 8 K_{\mathrm{IR}}$ and the implicit constant depending only on $d, \zeta, M, K_{\mathrm{IR}},s_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop prop_birth_sections of arXiv:2606.23648, '\textsc{Optimal Stopping for a Diffusion with Unobserved Bernoulli Drift}'] Under the standing assumptions of this section,
    \(
        x\in\mathcal I
    \)
    if and only if
    \begin{equation}
        \inf_{s>0}
        \left\{
            \frac12 k''(s)+G(s-x)k'(s)+c
        \right\}<0.
    \end{equation}
    Equivalently, under the assumption (ass_2 of the paper) 
    \begin{equation}
        x\in\mathcal I
        \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
        \exists\,s>0
        \text{ such that }
        G(s-x)<-P_k(s),
    \end{equation}
    with $P_k(\cdot)$ as in (equation Qk_def of the paper)
    Consequently, and with the convention \(\inf\emptyset=\infty\),
    \begin{equation}
        x_{birth}
        \coloneqq \inf \mathcal{I}
        =
        \inf\left\{
            x\ge0:
            \exists\,s>0
            \text{ such that }
            G(s-x)<-P_k(s)
        \right\}.
    \end{equation}

[Definition fractionalintegral of arXiv:2504.14457, 'Abstract'] The fractional integral, also known as the Riemann–Liouville integral, associates with a real function $ f:\mathbb {R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$  another function $I^{\alpha} f$ of the same kind for each value of the parameter $\alpha>0$
\begin{align}
    I^{\alpha}f(t)=\frac{1}{\Gamma(\alpha)} \int_0^t (t-\tau)^{\alpha-1}f(\tau)d\tau.
\end{align}

