[Definition Assumption \Unif\, of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] We say that $\mathcal{S}$ satisfies the assumption ${\mbox{\textsc{Unif(}$\delta$\textsc{)}}}\,={\mbox{\textsc{Unif(}$\delta,r_0,\theta_0$\textsc{)}}}\ $ for some parameters $\delta,r_0,\theta_0$ if all edge-lengths in $\mathcal{S}$ are comparable to $\delta$, meaning that for any  neighbouring $v^{\bullet}\in G^\bullet$ and $v^{\circ}\in G^\circ$ one has
	\begin{equation}
		r_0^{-1}\cdot \delta \leq  |\mathcal{S}(v^{\bullet})- \mathcal{S}(v^{\circ})| \leq r_0\cdot \delta,
	\end{equation}
and all the geometric angles in the quads $\mathcal{S}$ are bounded from below by $\theta_0$.

[Lemma seq of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] If $z=z_1-\epsilon$ for some suitable $\epsilon$ (say less or equal to $\frac{\mu_{n,N}}{2\kappa_{N}}$), then there exists a sequence $s_N$ with finite limit such that
    \begin{equation}
    \zeta^{\frac{3}{2}}(z)=\frac{\sqrt{\epsilon^3(z_{2}-z_{1})}}{2z_{1}}\left(1+\frac{3}{5}s_{N}\epsilon+O(\epsilon^2)\right)
\end{equation}
and hence,
\begin{equation}
   \kappa_{N}^{\frac{2}{3}}\zeta(z)=\frac{\epsilon\kappa_{N}}{\sigma_{n,N}}\left(1+\frac{2\epsilon s_{N}}{5}+O(\epsilon^2)\right),
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \left(\frac{\kappa_{N}}{\sigma^3_{n,N}}\right)^{\frac{1}{6}}\tilde{f}^{-\frac{1}{4}}(z)=1-\frac{2}{5} s_{N}\epsilon+O(\epsilon^2).
\end{equation}
Here $z_1$, $z_2$, $\kappa$ and $s$ are all double-indexed sequences with respect to $(j,k)\in\mathbb{N}^2$. We will abbreviate them as in this lemma if $(j,k)=(n,N)$. Moreover, the L--G transform $\zeta$ and induced $\tilde{f}$ is always assumed to be around $z_1$ (with parameter $(n,N)$ here).

