body:
[Lemma fixed-point-corep of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] Let $\varphi\colon H\to G$ be a homomorphism of compact Lie groups, let ${\mathcal{U}}_H$ be a complete $H$-universe, and let $V$ be any faithful $H$-representation. Then $\textbf{\textup{L}}(V,-)\times_\varphi G\in{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_\textup{$G$-gl}$ corepresents the composite
    \begin{equation}
        {{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_\textup{$G$-gl}\xrightarrow{\;\smash{\raisebox{-0.25ex}{\ensuremath{\scriptstyle\sim}}}\;}{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_\textup{$G$-gl}^\textup{closed}\xrightarrow{\;(-)^\varphi\circ\ev_{{\mathcal{U}}_H}\;}\Spc.
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, this functor is cocontinuous.
    \begin{proof}
        It is clear that $\textbf{\textup{L}}(V,-)\times_\varphi G$ is cofibrant and corepresents
        \begin{equation}
            \cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}\xrightarrow{\;\ev_V\;}\cat{$\bm{(H\times G)}$-Top}\xrightarrow{\;(-)^\varphi\;}\textbf{\textup{Top}}
        \end{equation}
        in the enriched sense. Corollary~(cor:corep-Top of the paper) therefore shows that it corepresents the right derived functor ${{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_\text{$G$-gl}\to\Spc$ of $((eq:fixed-points-corep-orthspc of the paper))$ in the $\infty$-categorical sense.

        To see that this right derived functor agrees with $((eq:orthspc-fixed-points-oo of the paper))$, let us fix an $H$-equivariant linear isometric embedding $i\colon V\to{\mathcal{U}}_H$. It will then suffice to show that for any cofibrant-fibrant orthogonal $G$-space $X$ the map $X(i)^\varphi\colon X(V)^\varphi\to X({\mathcal{U}}_H)^\varphi$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. For this, we pick an exhaustive filtration $i(V)\eqqcolon U_0\subset U_1\subset\cdots\subset{\mathcal{U}}_H$ of $U$ by finite-dimensional subrepresentations: for example, we can first choose a filtration by arbitrary finite subspaces $V_i$ and then inductively apply [schwede_orbispaces_2020]*{Proposition~A.7(i)} to find a finite-dimensional $G$-subrepresentation $U_n$ containing $V_n+U_{n-1}$. By fibrancy of $X$, each transition map $X(U_n)^\varphi\to X(U_{n+1})^\varphi$ is a weak homotopy equivalence, and it is moreover a closed embedding by cofibrancy. Thus, the induced map $X(V)^\varphi\cong X(U_0)^\varphi\to \mathop{\textup{colim}}_n X(U_n)^\varphi$ is again a weak homotopy equivalence. Moreover, [schwede2018global]*{Proposition~B.1(ii)} shows that the natural map from the right-hand side to $(\mathop{\textup{colim}}_n X(U_n))^\varphi$ is a homeomorphism, finishing the proof that $\textbf{\textup{L}}(V,-)/G$ corepresents $((eq:orthspc-fixed-points-oo of the paper))$.

        To prove that $((eq:orthspc-fixed-points-oo of the paper))$ is cocontinuous, it suffices to show that it preserves coproducts and pushouts. The first statement is clear, while for the second one it suffices by Lemma~(lemma:homotopy-po-equiv-fixed-points of the paper) that $\ev_{{\mathcal{U}}_H}\colon\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Top}\to\cat{$\bm{(H\times G)}$-Top}$ sends any pushout square
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                A\arrow[r,"i"]\arrow[d] & B\arrow[d]\\
                C\arrow[r] & D
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        where $i$ is a cofibration and $A,B,C,D$ are closed to a homotopy pushout in the $\mathcal G_{H,G}$-model structure. By Remark~(rk:level-cof-are-h-cof of the paper), $i$ is in particular an h-cofibration, hence so is $i({\mathcal{U}}_H)$ by [schwede2018global]*{Corollary~A.30(ii)}. The claim then follows from Lemma~(lemma:h-cof-compute-po-unbased of the paper).
    \end{proof}

[Theorem thm:alpha=0 of arXiv:2507.03552, 'On one-dimensional Cluster cluster model'] Let $d=1, \alpha = 0, 0<p<1$. $\forall x>0$:
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            \lim_{t \to \infty}\Prob{\frac{|\mathfs{C}_0(t)|}{\sqrt{t}} \le x}=2\Phi\rb{\frac{x(\eta-1)}{2}} - \frac{x(\eta-1)}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\frac{\big(\frac{x}{2}(\eta-1)\big)^2}{2}} - 1
        ,\end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
    where $\Phi$ is the cdf of a $N(0,1)$ and $\eta = p^{-1}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

